endobj
252 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (Medical imaging techniques like CT and MRI scans naturally capture internal anatomy as 2D projections, or shadows. If doctors want to analyze the complex blood flow inside a cerebral aneurysm, they usually have to convert these 2D scans into 3D grids first. This conversion step is known as tomographic reconstruction, and it creates a massive bottleneck before computationally heavy Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) simulations can even begin. Our project introduces a Physics- Informed Neural Network \(PINN\) designed to skip this reconstruction step entirely. By working solely with flattened 2D projections, our system can directly infer the hidden 3D hemodynamics, including continuous velocity and pressure fields. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (We built the architecture using two main mathematical components. The first is an 8-layer deep neural network acting as a continuous fluid solver. The second is a custom Forward Radon Transform module \(handling ray-casting\) that we engineered directly into the PyTorch dataloader. Rather than using traditional 3D data loss, our system takes its 3D volumetric predictions and dynamically squashes them into 2D shadows across the X, Y, and Z axes. At the same time, the Navier-Stokes equations constrain the network's spatial derivatives. This ensures the 3D geometry the AI guesses actually obeys the physical laws of mass and momentum. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 189 0 R
/E (To test if this could work in a real clinical setting, we ran extensive ablation and control studies using a cerebral aneurysm dataset. The system proved surprisingly data-efficient. It maintained physical accuracy even when we restricted it to just 16,000 spatial points. We also found that by applying adaptive loss weighting, the network acted as an advanced mathematical denoiser, effectively filtering out 10% Gaussian static. Finally, we addressed the mathematically ill-posed nature of single-axis projections by using a Stochastic Mean Projection across three different camera angles. This forced the AI to stop taking mathematical shortcuts and accurately reconstruct the true asymmetric 3D vortex. While currently an academic prototype running on consumer-grade hardware, our findings show that physics-driven deep learning can reconstruct 3D fluid mechanics straight from 2D clinical shadows. This sets a strong foundation for future tomography-free, real-time hemodynamic analysis in hospitals. )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
