379 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 196 0 R
/T (Tracking the exact blood velocity vectors and pressure distributions inside a cerebral aneurysm is an essential step in predicting if the vessel might rupture. Traditionally, biomechanical engineers and medical staff use Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) to map out these physical forces. The main problem is that standard CFD solvers are incredibly strict. They only work if you provide them with perfectly resolved, high-fidelity 3D spatial geometries and very specific boundary conditions. )
/E (Tracking the exact blood velocity vectors and pressure distributions inside a cerebral aneurysm is an essential step in predicting if the vessel might rupture. Traditionally, biomechanical engineers and medical staff use Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) to map out these physical forces. The main problem is that standard CFD solvers are incredibly strict. They only work if you provide them with perfectly resolved, high-fidelity 3D spatial geometries and very specific boundary conditions. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
380 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 196 0 R
/T (If hospitals want to run traditional CFD, they first have to mathematically "un-squash" hundreds of these 2D images to build a cohesive 3D model. This conversion process is known as Tomographic Reconstruction. It is notoriously slow, demands massive computing power, and often creates geometric artifacts that end up ruining the fluid simulations later in the pipeline. )
/E (If hospitals want to run traditional CFD, they first have to mathematically "un-squash" hundreds of these 2D images to build a cohesive 3D model. This conversion process is known as Tomographic Reconstruction. It is notoriously slow, demands massive computing power, and often creates geometric artifacts that end up ruining the fluid simulations later in the pipeline. )
/Pg 19 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
