384 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (Research into computational hemodynamics and machine learning generally falls into four main areas, each with its own pros and cons: )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
385 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (\(1\) Traditional Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\): Classical solvers use finite element or finite volume methods to generate incredibly accurate simulations. However, their mathematical rigidity is a drawback. They require massive computing clusters and perfect 3D boundary definitions, which doctors rarely have time to generate in an emergency setting. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
386 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (\(2\) Pure Data-Driven Deep Learning: Early attempts to speed up these simulations relied on standard Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNNs\) to predict flow fields purely from visual data. The issue here is that when given sparse or noisy hospital data, these models act like simple image guessers. They produce outputs that might look correct to the human eye but completely violate the laws of mass and momentum conservation. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
387 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 197 0 R
/E (\(3\) Physics-Informed Neural Networks \(PINNs\): First introduced by Raissi et al., PINNs solve the physics-violation issue by calculating the partial differential equations \(PDEs\) of fluid flow during the training phase itself. A specific framework called Hidden Fluid Mechanics \(HFM\) proved that PINNs can accurately infer unmeasured velocity and pressure fields just by watching how a medical contrast dye moves over time. )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
388 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 197 0 R
/T (1.2 Brief Research in the Domain )
/E (1.2 Brief Research in the Domain )
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
