421 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(2\) Mathematical Denoising: We show that placing a heavy mathematical weight on the Navier- Stokes equations stops the network from memorizing noisy data. In short, we use the laws of physics as an active, intelligent filter against background static. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
422 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(3\) Stochastic Mean Ray-Casting: Running complex Radon Transformations usually demands massive amounts of VRAM, which consumer-grade hardware doesn't have. To get around this, we built a statistical sampling method. It computes the shadows accurately using random mini-batches so the GPU memory doesn't crash. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
423 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(4\) Transparent Failure Analysis: We actively document how and why the network fails when given only a single-angle projection. Pointing out this physical collapse highlights the true mathematical boundaries of the problem and proves exactly why you have to use multiple angles for this to work. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
424 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (This project brings a few new technical ideas to the table, connecting theoretical physics with applied computer science at the undergraduate level: )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
425 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(1\) Tomography-Free Inference: Standard medical setups require tomographic reconstruction before anything else. Our system skips this by using a dynamic 2D projection loss to figure out 3D fluid details. We essentially force the network to understand 3D depth by continually checking its work against flat 2D shadows. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
426 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(2\) Mathematical Denoising: We show that placing a heavy mathematical weight on the Navier- Stokes equations stops the network from memorizing noisy data. In short, we use the laws of physics as an active, intelligent filter against background static. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
427 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(3\) Stochastic Mean Ray-Casting: Running complex Radon Transformations usually demands massive amounts of VRAM, which consumer-grade hardware doesn't have. To get around this, we built a statistical sampling method. It computes the shadows accurately using random mini-batches so the GPU memory doesn't crash. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
428 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (\(4\) Transparent Failure Analysis: We actively document how and why the network fails when given only a single-angle projection. Pointing out this physical collapse highlights the true mathematical boundaries of the problem and proves exactly why you have to use multiple angles for this to work. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
