/P 203 0 R
/T (perfect 3D point cloud. In a real-world clinic, you almost never have that kind of perfect 3D data, which creates a major roadblock for deploying this tech. )
/E (perfect 3D point cloud. In a real-world clinic, you almost never have that kind of perfect 3D data, which creates a major roadblock for deploying this tech. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 203 0 R
/T (The problem is that doing this with only a few camera angles is an ill-posed inverse problem. To get a good 3D shape, you need hundreds of camera angles, which means a lot of radiation. If you limit the angles or the X-ray is noisy, the reconstruction creates awful geometric artifacts like blurring and streaking. If you try to run a fluid simulation on top of a blurry 3D model, you will completely ruin the results. )
/E (The problem is that doing this with only a few camera angles is an ill-posed inverse problem. To get a good 3D shape, you need hundreds of camera angles, which means a lot of radiation. If you limit the angles or the X-ray is noisy, the reconstruction creates awful geometric artifacts like blurring and streaking. If you try to run a fluid simulation on top of a blurry 3D model, you will completely ruin the results. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 203 0 R
/T (Even though HFM works mathematically, everyday medical scanners do not capture perfect 3D point clouds. Hardware like CT scanners and X-ray angiography machines actually capture line integrals. When an X-ray shoots through a patient, different tissues and blood absorb different amounts of radiation. The detector plate simply records the total absorption along that beam's path. This process squashes actual 3D depth down into a flat 2D shadow. )
/E (Even though HFM works mathematically, everyday medical scanners do not capture perfect 3D point clouds. Hardware like CT scanners and X-ray angiography machines actually capture line integrals. When an X-ray shoots through a patient, different tissues and blood absorb different amounts of radiation. The detector plate simply records the total absorption along that beam's path. This process squashes actual 3D depth down into a flat 2D shadow. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 203 0 R
/T (In math, we call this projection process the Forward Radon Transform. If a hospital wants to use standard CFD or HFM, they have to reverse this process. They run algorithms like Filtered Back Projection \(FBP\) or Algebraic Reconstruction Techniques \(ART\) to "un-squash" the 2D shadows back into a 3D grid. We call this Tomographic Reconstruction. )
/E (In math, we call this projection process the Forward Radon Transform. If a hospital wants to use standard CFD or HFM, they have to reverse this process. They run algorithms like Filtered Back Projection \(FBP\) or Algebraic Reconstruction Techniques \(ART\) to "un-squash" the 2D shadows back into a 3D grid. We call this Tomographic Reconstruction. )
/Pg 26 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
