453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (This chapter covered the history of computational hemodynamics. We looked at how the field moved from rigid CFD solvers to using Physics-Informed Neural Networks \(PINNs\). The literature shows that Hidden Fluid Mechanics \(HFM\) does a great job pulling 3D fluid parameters from simple dye concentrations. Even so, a big clinical gap remains. Medical scanners capture flat 2D line integrals via the Forward Radon Transform, making direct 3D inference incredibly difficult. We reviewed recent studies that put projection operators straight into neural loss functions. This background research theoretically supports our project's main approach: using a dynamic 2D projection loss to bypass )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
