/E (2.6 Identified Gap Addressed )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
456 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (People have theorized about combining line-integral projection loss with PINNs for acoustics and general computer vision. However, applying this idea to the messy, nonlinear Navier-Stokes equations for 3D cerebral aneurysms is completely uncharted territory. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
457 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 204 0 R
/T (2.5 Integrating Projections with Neural Networks )
/E (2.5 Integrating Projections with Neural Networks )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
458 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (To get around this reconstruction bottleneck, recent computer science studies started mixing deep learning directly with projection data. Rather than trying to build the 3D shape first, researchers built hybrid models. These models put the Forward Radon Transform right inside the neural network's loss function. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
459 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (It works a lot like Neural Radiance Fields \(NeRFs\). In a NeRF, the AI learns 3D volumes just by casting rays and rendering 2D images. When you embed ray-casting into the learning process, the network has to make sure its internal 3D guesses actually match the real 2D shadows measured by the scanner. Other scientific fields are already doing this. Some teams used line-integral loss to rebuild 3D acoustic fields from optical sound projections. This proves that deep neural networks can figure out 3D physical traits without relying on outdated inverse solvers. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
460 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (Right now, the literature does not offer a solid way to figure out 3D cardiovascular pressure and velocity using nothing but 2D orthographic shadows. Our project targets this exact gap. We built a dynamic, stochastic Ray-Casting projection loss that trains an HFM setup using only 2D shadows. The goal is to skip traditional tomographic reconstruction completely. Doing this avoids those ugly geometric artifacts and speeds up the transition from taking a clinical image to getting a usable fluid analysis. )
/Pg 27 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
