565 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 209 0 R
/T (Computational Limitations & HPC Deferment: )
/E (Computational Limitations & HPC Deferment: )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [15]
>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E ( We have completely written and validated the code for this multi-angle extension, but actually running it is still ongoing. To calculate the 2nd-order Navier-Stokes spatial derivatives, you have to keep the entire 3D computational graph in active VRAM. When you expand the projection graph from one camera to three, the memory requirement explodes. During our test runs on consumer-grade GPUs with 6GB to 8GB of VRAM, this instantly triggered CUDA Out-Of-Memory \(OOM\) crashes every single time. Because of this hardware limit, we had to pause the physical training of the multi- angle network. It is ready and waiting to be deployed on a High-Performance Computing \(HPC\) cluster in the future. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [16]
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E (When we ran early tests using just a single-axis projection \(looking only down the Z-axis\), we realized it was a flawed inverse problem. A single camera angle erases so much spatial depth that the neural network can cheat. It minimizes the data loss by drawing featureless pressure gradients and completely symmetric velocity spirals. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [17]
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E (To shut down this mathematical loophole, we built a Multi-Angle Orthogonal Projection method into the code. Instead of looking from one angle, the system generates three different ray identifiers at the exact same time: )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [18]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 209 0 R
/E (By checking the Data Loss across all three shadows at once, we severely limit the network's options. In theory, this forces the AI to build a continuous 3D geometry that obeys the heavy Navier-Stokes equations and perfectly casts three different, asymmetrical 2D shadows at the same time. )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [19]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 209 0 R
/T (3.5 The Proposed Multi-Angle Orthogonal Extension \(Ongoing Work\) )
/E (3.5 The Proposed Multi-Angle Orthogonal Extension \(Ongoing Work\) )
/Pg 32 0 R
/K [20]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
