<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 211 0 R
/T (CHAPTER 4 - RESULTS AND ANALYSIS )
/E (CHAPTER 4 - RESULTS AND ANALYSIS )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 211 0 R
/T (4.1 Dataset and Experimental Setup )
/E (4.1 Dataset and Experimental Setup )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (We trained and tested our network using the Aneurysm3D.mat point cloud. This dataset contains scattered spatial coordinates that mimic complex blood flow in the brain. We wanted to make sure our approach could actually run in a fast, local clinic, so we purposefully built and ran all models on standard consumer-grade GPUs. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 211 0 R
/T (4.2 Architecture Optimization and Physical Robustness )
/E (4.2 Architecture Optimization and Physical Robustness )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Before we jumped into the incredibly difficult job of guessing 3D shapes from 2D projections, we ran a series of ablation studies on the fully resolved 3D dataset. We needed to establish strong baseline metrics and make sure the base neural network could handle the physics. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (We started with a simple 4-layer architecture. This early model did fine mapping out basic concentration gradients, but it completely failed to capture the highly nonlinear Navier-Stokes equations. As a result, it could not reconstruct the fluid vortex. Basically, a 4-layer network just does not have the mathematical depth to handle the complicated spatial derivatives of 3D blood flow. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (To fix this, we upgraded the network to 8 hidden layers, giving each layer 256 neurons. This gave the system the computing power it needed. The larger network successfully brought the validation loss down for all the hidden variables, giving us an accurate map of the continuous velocity and pressure fields. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 211 0 R
/E (Running on normal hardware meant we had to manage memory very carefully. We set the network to use a stochastic batch size of 5,000 spatial points for each training step. This kept our VRAM usage in check while still giving us enough data points to track the continuous fluid motion accurately. Throughout the process, we kept a close eye on network performance, loss curves, and gradients using Weights & Biases \(W&B\) for real-time telemetry. )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 211 0 R
/T (4.2.1 Experiment 1: Baseline vs. Capacity Upgrade \(Hyperparameter Tuning\) )
/E (4.2.1 Experiment 1: Baseline vs. Capacity Upgrade \(Hyperparameter Tuning\) )
/Pg 34 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
