/E (We wanted to fix the severe depth-loss problem we saw in Experiment 6, so we upgraded the code to support Multi-Angle Orthogonal Projections. This new method looks at the Top-Down, Side-Profile, and Front-Profile shadows all at the same time. This traps the network and stops it from taking those "lazy" symmetric shortcuts. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
623 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (We fully wrote and engineered this solution in the code, but we haven't been able to run the final experiment yet because of major hardware limits. Calculating the 2nd-order Navier-Stokes spatial derivatives means the GPU has to hold the entire 3D computational graph in active memory. When you jump from processing one camera angle to three angles at the same time, the memory requirement skyrockets. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
624 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 216 0 R
/E (We tried running this on standard consumer GPUs that have 6GB to 8GB of VRAM, but the workload instantly caused CUDA Out-Of-Memory \(OOM\) crashes every single time. Because our randomized ray-casting changes dynamically, standard VRAM caching tricks did not work. So, even though the multi-angle math works perfectly in theory to solve the single-angle problem, we had to pause the actual physical training. We are waiting to get time on a High-Performance Computing \(HPC\) cluster to finish this step. )
/Pg 39 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
