632 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (In this project, we built a Physics-Informed Neural Network \(PINN\) to skip the slow and expensive tomographic reconstruction step normally used in clinical fluid dynamics. We did this by feeding the nonlinear Navier-Stokes equations right into our deep learning architecture. This allowed the system to figure out continuous 3D hemodynamics using nothing but flat, 2D data. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
633 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (We ran 11 specific experiments to test how well this works. Our first few tests \(Experiments 1 through 3\) checked the base 8-layer network's physical limits. Even when we cut the data down to just 16,000 spatial points, the network maintained its accuracy. This proved that using a physical loss function actually works like a bridge to fill in missing data. When we ran the zero-physics control study, the network turned into a basic image-regressor and drew impossible, decoupled flow lines. This confirmed we absolutely need the Navier-Stokes equations to get real results. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
634 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
635 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (After proving the network was stable, we moved on to the main problem: guessing 3D shapes from 2D data. We wrote a stochastic mean ray-casting algorithm inside PyTorch to squash the 3D predictions into flat clinical shadows \(the Forward Radon Transform\). In Experiment 10, we used a single-axis projection. The loss numbers looked good, but the actual output showed a major flaw. Using only one shadow destroys too much spatial depth. The AI cheated by drawing basic, featureless gradients and perfectly symmetric spirals instead of the real aneurysm vortex. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
636 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (To close this mathematical loophole, we wrote the code for a multi-angle orthogonal projection setup \(Experiment 11\). However, we hit a hard hardware limit during testing. Calculating the 2nd-order spatial derivatives for three cameras at once requires way too much memory. It caused constant CUDA Out-Of-Memory \(OOM\) crashes on our consumer-grade GPUs. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
637 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (In the end, this project shows exactly where single-angle tomographic inference fails. We built a working, physics-driven codebase that successfully blends deep learning with fluid mechanics. It is completely ready to be deployed on a high-performance computer that can handle the multi-angle math. )
/Pg 41 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
