639 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (Even though our math works on simulated data, we need to make several upgrades before doctors can actually use this as a daily tool. Here are the main areas for future research: )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
640 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (\(1\) HPC Deployment for Multi-Angle Orthogonal Inference First, we need to run our Multi-Angle code on a High-Performance Computing \(HPC\) cluster. Consumer GPUs only have 6 to 8GB of VRAM, which is simply not enough. An HPC cluster will let the network calculate the massive gradient graphs needed to cast X, Y, and Z shadows all at the same time. This should shut down the loopholes we saw in Experiment 10 and force the AI to rebuild the correct, asymmetrical 3D vortex. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
641 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
642 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (\(3\) Pulsatile Flow and Time-Varying Boundaries Blood does not flow at a constant rate; it pulses with the heartbeat. Our current setup assumes a steady flow. Later versions of this PINN will need to handle dynamic, changing boundary conditions. This will let the network pinpoint the exact moment of maximum systolic pressure, which is the exact fraction of a second when an aneurysm is most likely to rupture. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
643 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (\(4\) Advanced Neural Architectures to Combat Spectral Bias Standard Multi-Layer Perceptrons \(MLPs\) have a known issue called "spectral bias," which means they struggle to learn very sharp, high-frequency details. To map out extreme velocity changes near the vessel walls, we should swap the MLP for Fourier Feature Networks \(FFNs\) or Sinusoidal Representation Networks \(SIRENs\). These architectures are much better at handling fine physics details in continuous environments. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
644 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (\(5\) Hybrid Sensor Fusion for Hyper-Accuracy Future setups could combine different types of data. If we mix high-resolution 2D X-rays with sparse, low-resolution 3D points from a 4D Flow MRI, the network could use the best parts of both scanners at the same time. This hybrid approach would anchor the AI's guesses and create an incredibly accurate hemodynamic model. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
645 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (\(6\) Clinical User Interface and API Deployment To make this actually useful for doctors, we have to wrap the PyTorch engine into a backend REST API. We could build a frontend dashboard where radiologists simply upload their standard 2D X-rays. The cloud would process the physics and send back an interactive 3D map showing the velocity streamlines and dangerous pressure zones in real time. )
/Pg 42 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
