1861 0 obj
<<
/Title (Tracking the exact blood velocity vectors and pressure distributions inside a cerebral aneurysm is an essential step in predicting if the vessel might rupture. Traditionally, biomechanical engineers and medical staff use Computational Fluid Dynamics \(CFD\) to map out these physical forces. The main problem is that standard CFD solvers are incredibly strict. They only work if you provide them with perfectly resolved, high-fidelity 3D spatial geometries and very specific boundary conditions.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1856 0 R
/Next 1868 0 R
>>
endobj
1862 0 obj
<<
/Title (If hospitals want to run traditional CFD, they first have to mathematically "un-squash" hundreds of these 2D images to build a cohesive 3D model. This conversion process is known as Tomographic Reconstruction. It is notoriously slow, demands massive computing power, and often creates geometric artifacts that end up ruining the fluid simulations later in the pipeline.)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1856 0 R
/Prev 1868 0 R
>>
endobj
1863 0 obj
<<
/Title (\(4\) Tomographic Reconstruction Constraints: Current hospital pipelines rely heavily on techniques like Filtered Back Projection \(FBP\) or Algebraic Reconstruction Techniques \(ART\) to build 3D shapes from 2D X-rays. These methods require high doses of radiation to capture enough angles and tend to struggle heavily with signal noise.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1857 0 R
/Next 1864 0 R
>>
endobj
1864 0 obj
<<
/Title (The Domain Overlap: PINNs have been massively successful in fully resolved 3D environments. However, trying to apply PINNs directly to 2D projections to figure out both the 3D geometry and the fluid physics at the exact same time is a highly complex inverse problem that hasn't been explored much.)
/Dest [21 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1857 0 R
/Prev 1863 0 R
>>
endobj
