>>
endobj
1894 0 obj
<<
/Title (When it comes to scientific machine learning, Physics-Informed Neural Networks \(PINNs\) changed everything. Raissi, Perdikaris, and Karniadakis formally introduced them in 2019. Standard neural networks act like data-driven black boxes. They map inputs to outputs without actually understanding the physical world. As a result, if you give them sparse or noisy data, they spit out physically impossible predictions.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1889 0 R
/Next 1895 0 R
>>
endobj
1895 0 obj
<<
/Title (PINNs fix this. They act as universal function approximators that weave physical laws right into the learning phase. We usually express these laws as partial differential equations \(PDEs\). In fluid mechanics, we use the Navier-Stokes equations to enforce the conservation of mass, momentum, and energy.)
/Dest [24 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1889 0 R
/Prev 1894 0 R
>>
endobj
1896 0 obj
<<
/Title <>
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1886 0 R
/Prev 1890 0 R
/Next 1897 0 R
>>
endobj
1897 0 obj
<<
/Title (Researchers proved HFM handles noisy, low-resolution data exceptionally well. But there is a catch. The original HFM framework assumes you already have the concentration data mapped out as a)
/Dest [25 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1886 0 R
/Prev 1896 0 R
/Next 1891 0 R
>>
endobj
1898 0 obj
<<
/Title (Even though HFM works mathematically, everyday medical scanners do not capture perfect 3D point clouds. Hardware like CT scanners and X-ray angiography machines actually capture line integrals. When an X-ray shoots through a patient, different tissues and blood absorb different amounts of radiation. The detector plate simply records the total absorption along that beam's path. This process squashes actual 3D depth down into a flat 2D shadow.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1892 0 R
/Next 1902 0 R
>>
endobj
1899 0 obj
<<
/Title (The problem is that doing this with only a few camera angles is an ill-posed inverse problem. To get a good 3D shape, you need hundreds of camera angles, which means a lot of radiation. If you limit the angles or the X-ray is noisy, the reconstruction creates awful geometric artifacts like blurring and streaking. If you try to run a fluid simulation on top of a blurry 3D model, you will completely ruin the results.)
/Dest [26 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1892 0 R
/Prev 1902 0 R
>>
endobj
1900 0 obj
<<
/Title (2.5 Integrating Projections with Neural Networks)
/Dest [27 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 7 0 R
/Prev 1892 0 R
/Next 1903 0 R
>>
endobj
1901 0 obj
